<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-equations-of-tangents" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Equations of Tangents</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.2 Differentiation I
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Determine the equations of tangents to a curve
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To discover the tangent line formula by working with <m>f(x) = x^2</m>,
                    building fluency with the connection between the derivative and the gradient of the tangent
                    before encountering the general formula.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Circulate during Part A — most learners will complete the table independently.
                    For Part B(a), prompt: <em>"If the gradient is zero, what does the tangent look like?
                    What does that tell you about the curve at that point?"</em>
                    For Part C, encourage learners to verify each answer by substituting the given point
                    into their tangent equation.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="activity-tangent-parabola">
        <title>Tangent Lines on a Parabola</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. By the power rule, <m>f'(x) = 2x</m>.
            </p>

            <paragraphs>
                <title>Part A: Building a Table</title>
                <p>
                    At <m>x = 1</m>: the curve passes through the point <m>(1,\, 1)</m>
                    and the gradient there is <m>f'(1) = 2</m>.
                    Using the point-slope form <m>y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)</m>,
                    write the equation of the line through <m>(1, 1)</m> with gradient <m>2</m>.
                    Simplify to the form <m>y = mx + c</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now do the same for <m>x = 2</m>, then use that method to complete the full table.
                    For each row, the curve passes through <m>(a,\, f(a))</m> with gradient <m>f'(a)</m>.
                </p>
                <table xml:id="table-tangent-parabola">
                    <title>Tangent lines to <m>f(x) = x^2</m></title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                            <cell><m>a</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f(a) = a^2</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f'(a) = 2a</m></cell>
                            <cell>Tangent equation</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <solution component="lesson-plan">
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                            <cell><m>a</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f(a)</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>f'(a)</m></cell>
                            <cell>Tangent equation</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>-4</m></cell><cell><m>y = -4x - 4</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>y = -2x - 1</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>y = 0</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>y = 2x - 1</m></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>2</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>4</m></cell><cell><m>y = 4x - 4</m></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </solution>
            </paragraphs>

            <paragraphs>
                <title>Part B: Observations and the General Formula</title>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the tangent line at <m>x = 0</m>?
                            What does a horizontal tangent tell you about the shape of the curve at that point?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                The tangent is <m>y = 0</m> (the <m>x</m>-axis).
                                A horizontal tangent means the gradient is zero — the curve is neither increasing
                                nor decreasing there. This is the minimum of the parabola.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Compare the tangents at <m>x = 1</m> and <m>x = -1</m>. What do you notice?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                They have equal and opposite gradients (<m>+2</m> and <m>-2</m>).
                                The parabola is symmetric about the <m>y</m>-axis, so the tangents
                                are mirror images of each other.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The method you used in Part A works for any curve, not just <m>f(x) = x^2</m>.
                            For a general function <m>f</m>, the tangent at <m>x = a</m> passes through
                            the point <m>(a, f(a))</m> with gradient <m>f'(a)</m>.
                            Apply the point-slope form to write a general formula for this tangent line.
                            Simplify.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Point <m>(a, f(a))</m>, gradient <m>f'(a)</m>, point-slope form:
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>y - f(a) \amp= f'(a)(x - a)</mrow>
                                    <mrow>y \amp= f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)</mrow>
                                </md>
                                For <m>f(x) = x^2</m> this gives <m>y = 2a(x-a) + a^2 = 2ax - a^2</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                This is the key moment: learners have just derived the tangent line formula themselves.
                                Emphasise that it is nothing new — just point-slope form applied to what they already know.
                                For <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, point out the elegant structure: gradient <m>2a</m>,
                                <m>y</m>-intercept <m>-a^2</m> — the negative of the <m>y</m>-coordinate of the
                                point of tangency.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>

            <paragraphs>
                <title>Part C: Working Backwards</title>
                <ol marker="(a)" start="4">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Use your formula from (c) to find the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2</m>
                            that has gradient <m>6</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                We need <m>f'(a) = 2a = 6</m>, so <m>a = 3</m>.
                                The tangent is <m>y = 6x - 9</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find <em>all</em> tangent lines to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> that pass through
                            the point <m>(0, -4)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                The tangent at <m>x = a</m> is <m>y = 2ax - a^2</m>.
                                For it to pass through <m>(0, -4)</m>, substitute <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = -4</m>:
                                <md>-4 = 2a(0) - a^2 = -a^2 \implies a^2 = 4 \implies a = \pm 2</md>
                                Two tangent lines:
                                <ul>
                                    <li>At <m>x = 2</m>: <m>y = 4x - 4</m></li>
                                    <li>At <m>x = -2</m>: <m>y = -4x - 4</m></li>
                                </ul>
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                The point <m>(0, -4)</m> lies <em>below</em> the parabola, so two tangents exist.
                                A point <em>inside</em> the parabola — such as <m>(0, 1)</m> — gives <m>a^2 = -1</m>,
                                which has no real solution: no tangent from that point can reach the curve.
                                This is a powerful geometric insight.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        10 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        Formalise the tangent line formula and the step-by-step procedure
                        that learners used in the activity.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Gradient = derivative at the point.</alert>
                        The gradient of the tangent at <m>x = a</m> is <m>f'(a)</m>.
                        Learners already applied this in every row of the table.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>The tangent line formula</alert> follows from the point-slope form with
                        point <m>(a, f(a))</m> and gradient <m>f'(a)</m>:
                        <md>y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Horizontal tangents</alert> occur when <m>f'(a) = 0</m>.
                        These identify peaks and troughs — foreshadowing the stationary points work in later sections.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <alert>Working backwards</alert> (Part C) — finding the point of tangency from
                        information about the line — is a key exam skill. Walk through Part C(e) again
                        as a class if time allows.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <p>
        A <term>tangent</term> to a curve at a point is the straight line that touches the curve at
        exactly that point, sharing the same gradient as the curve there.
        Since the derivative <m>f'(a)</m> gives the gradient of <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>,
        we can write the tangent using the point-slope form of a line with point <m>(a, f(a))</m>:
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="insight-tangent-formula">
        <title>Equation of a Tangent Line</title>
        <p>
            The tangent to the curve <m>y = f(x)</m> at the point <m>x = a</m> is:
            <md>y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)</md>
            where <m>f'(a)</m> is the gradient of the curve at that point and
            <m>\bigl(a,\, f(a)\bigr)</m> is the point of tangency.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <insight xml:id="insight-tangent-procedure">
        <title>How to Find a Tangent Line</title>
        <p>Given <m>f(x)</m>, to find the tangent at <m>x = a</m>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Differentiate to find <m>f'(x)</m>, then evaluate at <m>x = a</m>
                    to get the gradient <m>m = f'(a)</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Evaluate the function at <m>x = a</m> to find the point <m>(a, f(a))</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>Substitute into <m>y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)</m> and simplify.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-tangent-concept">
        <caption>
            The tangent to <m>y = f(x)</m> at the point <m>(a, f(a))</m> touches the curve there with gradient <m>f'(a)</m>. Drag <m>P</m> along the curve and watch the tangent rotate and its equation update.
        </caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-tangent-explorer" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/tangent-explorer.js">
            <slate xml:id="tangent-explorer" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>Drag <m>P</m> along <m>f(x) = x^2</m>. The gradient is <m>f'(a) = 2a</m>, and the tangent's equation <m>y = 2a\,x - a^2</m> updates as you move.</p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-tangent-quadratic">
        <title>Tangent to a Quadratic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2 - 4x</m> at <m>x = 3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1: Find the gradient.</alert>
                By the power rule, <m>f'(x) = 2x - 4</m>.
                At <m>x = 3</m>:
                <md>f'(3) = 2(3) - 4 = 2</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2: Find the point.</alert>
                <md>f(3) = 3^2 - 4(3) = 9 - 12 = -3</md>
                The point of tangency is <m>(3, -3)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3: Write the tangent.</alert>
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp= f'(3)(x - 3) + f(3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 2(x - 3) + (-3)</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 2x - 6 - 3</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 2x - 9</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-tangent-cubic">
        <title>Tangent to a Cubic</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^3 - 2x</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 1:</alert>
                <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 2</m>, so <m>f'(2) = 3(4) - 2 = 10</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 2:</alert>
                <m>f(2) = 8 - 4 = 4</m>. Point of tangency: <m>(2, 4)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <alert>Step 3:</alert>
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp= 10(x - 2) + 4</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 10x - 20 + 4</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 10x - 16</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-tangent-parallel">
        <title>Finding the Point of Tangency from the Gradient</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the equation of the tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2 + 3x</m> that is
                parallel to the line <m>y = 7x - 1</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                A line parallel to <m>y = 7x - 1</m> has gradient <m>7</m>.
                We need the point where <m>f'(a) = 7</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>f'(x) = 2x + 3</m>:
                <md>2a + 3 = 7 \implies a = 2</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                At <m>x = 2</m>: <m>f(2) = 4 + 6 = 10</m>. Point of tangency: <m>(2, 10)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The tangent with gradient <m>7</m> through <m>(2, 10)</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>y \amp= 7(x - 2) + 10</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 7x - 14 + 10</mrow>
                    <mrow>\amp= 7x - 4</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <exercises>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-basic">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equation of the tangent to each curve at the given point.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = 2x^2</m> at <m>x = 3</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^3 + 1</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = 5x^2 - x + 4</m> at <m>x = 2</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2</m> at <m>x = 1</m></p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 4x</m>, <m>f'(3) = 12</m>.
                            <m>f(3) = 18</m>. Point: <m>(3, 18)</m>.
                            <md>y = 12(x-3) + 18 = 12x - 18</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m>, <m>f'(2) = 12</m>.
                            <m>f(2) = 9</m>. Point: <m>(2, 9)</m>.
                            <md>y = 12(x-2) + 9 = 12x - 15</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 10x - 1</m>, <m>f'(2) = 19</m>.
                            <m>f(2) = 22</m>. Point: <m>(2, 22)</m>.
                            <md>y = 19(x-2) + 22 = 19x - 16</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 6x</m>, <m>f'(1) = 3 - 6 = -3</m>.
                            <m>f(1) = 1 - 3 = -2</m>. Point: <m>(1, -2)</m>.
                            <md>y = -3(x-1) + (-2) = -3x + 1</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><m>y = 12x - 18</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = 12x - 15</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = 19x - 16</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = -3x + 1</m></li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-horizontal">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the coordinates of all points on the curve where the tangent is horizontal.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^2 - 6x + 5</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^3 - 12x</m></p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 2x - 6 = 0 \implies x = 3</m>.
                            <m>f(3) = 9 - 18 + 5 = -4</m>. Point: <m>(3, -4)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 3x^2 - 12 = 0 \implies x^2 = 4 \implies x = \pm 2</m>.
                            <m>f(2) = 8 - 24 = -16</m> and <m>f(-2) = -8 + 24 = 16</m>.
                            Points: <m>(2, -16)</m> and <m>(-2, 16)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                    <li><m>(3, -4)</m></li>
                    <li><m>(2, -16)</m> and <m>(-2, 16)</m></li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-parallel">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equation of the tangent to the given curve that is parallel to the given line.
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^2</m>, parallel to <m>y = 4x + 1</m></p></li>
                    <li><p><m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x</m>, parallel to <m>y = 9x</m></p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Need <m>f'(a) = 4</m>: <m>2a = 4 \implies a = 2</m>.
                            <m>f(2) = 4</m>. Tangent:
                            <md>y = 4(x-2) + 4 = 4x - 4</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Need <m>f'(a) = 9</m>: <m>3a^2 - 3 = 9 \implies a^2 = 4 \implies a = \pm 2</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = 2</m>: <m>f(2) = 8 - 6 = 2</m>.
                            <md>y = 9(x-2) + 2 = 9x - 16</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            At <m>x = -2</m>: <m>f(-2) = -8 + 6 = -2</m>.
                            <md>y = 9(x+2) + (-2) = 9x + 16</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            There are two tangents: <m>y = 9x - 16</m> and <m>y = 9x + 16</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><m>y = 4x - 4</m></li>
                    <li><m>y = 9x - 16</m> (at <m>x = 2</m>) and <m>y = 9x + 16</m> (at <m>x = -2</m>)</li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-find-k" original="PRECALC/modules/m49455/index.cnxml" license="CC-BY-4.0">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all values of <m>k</m> such that the line <m>y = 4x - 9</m> is tangent to
                    <m>f(x) = x^2 - kx</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <p>
                    The tangent at <m>x = a</m> must satisfy two conditions simultaneously:
                    the gradient matches and the line passes through the point on the curve.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <alert>Gradient condition:</alert> <m>f'(x) = 2x - k</m>, so <m>f'(a) = 2a - k = 4</m>,
                    giving <m>k = 2a - 4</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <alert>Point condition:</alert> The point <m>(a, a^2 - ka)</m> lies on <m>y = 4x - 9</m>:
                    <md>a^2 - ka = 4a - 9</md>
                    Substituting <m>k = 2a - 4</m>:
                    <md>
                        <mrow>a^2 - (2a-4)a \amp= 4a - 9</mrow>
                        <mrow>a^2 - 2a^2 + 4a \amp= 4a - 9</mrow>
                        <mrow>-a^2 \amp= -9</mrow>
                        <mrow>a \amp= \pm 3</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    When <m>a = 3</m>: <m>k = 2(3) - 4 = 2</m>.
                    When <m>a = -3</m>: <m>k = 2(-3) - 4 = -10</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>k = 2</m> or <m>k = -10</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-external-point">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the equations of all tangent lines to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> that pass through
                    the point <m>(2, 0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    Write the tangent at a general point <m>x = a</m> (as in Part B of the activity),
                    then substitute <m>(2, 0)</m>.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <p>
                    The tangent to <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at <m>x = a</m> is <m>y = 2ax - a^2</m>
                    (from Part B of the activity).
                    For it to pass through <m>(2, 0)</m>:
                    <md>0 = 2a(2) - a^2 = 4a - a^2 = a(4 - a)</md>
                    So <m>a = 0</m> or <m>a = 4</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    At <m>a = 0</m>: the tangent is <m>y = 0</m> — the <m>x</m>-axis itself.
                </p>
                <p>
                    At <m>a = 4</m>: <m>f(4) = 16</m>, and the tangent is:
                    <md>y = 8(x - 4) + 16 = 8x - 16</md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Two tangents: <m>y = 0</m> and <m>y = 8x - 16</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>y = 0</m> (the <m>x</m>-axis, tangent at <m>x = 0</m>)
                    and <m>y = 8x - 16</m> (tangent at <m>x = 4</m>).
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-velocity">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A stone is thrown upward from ground level. Its height after <m>t</m> seconds is
                    <md>h(t) = 30t - 5t^2 \text{ metres.}</md>
                </p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the velocity of the stone at <m>t = 2</m>.</p></li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Write the equation of the tangent to the graph of <m>h(t)</m> at <m>t = 2</m>.
                            What does this line represent physically?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li><p>At what time does the stone reach its maximum height? What is that height?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>h'(t) = 30 - 10t</m>, so <m>h'(2) = 30 - 20 = 10</m> m/s.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>h(2) = 60 - 20 = 40</m>. Point: <m>(2, 40)</m>.
                            <md>y = 10(t - 2) + 40 = 10t + 20</md>
                            This line represents the height the stone <em>would</em> reach if it maintained
                            its speed at <m>t = 2</m> — that is, if gravity suddenly stopped acting on it
                            from that moment.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Maximum height when <m>h'(t) = 0</m>:
                            <md>30 - 10t = 0 \implies t = 3 \text{ s}</md>
                            <md>h(3) = 90 - 45 = 45 \text{ m}</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><m>10</m> m/s</li>
                    <li><m>y = 10t + 20</m>; the height if the stone continued at its speed at <m>t=2</m></li>
                    <li>Maximum at <m>t = 3</m> s; height <m>= 45</m> m</li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-cubic-reintersect">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The tangent to <m>f(x) = x^3</m> at the point <m>x = a</m> meets the curve again
                    at a second point. The case <m>a = 1</m> is shown below.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-cubic-tangent">
                    <caption>
                        The tangent to <m>y = x^3</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> is the line <m>y = 3x - 2</m>.
                        It meets the cubic again at <m>(-2, -8)</m>.
                    </caption>
                    <image xml:id="tikz-cubic-tangent" width="55%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Graph of y equals x cubed with a straight orange tangent line at the
                            point (1,1). The tangent crosses the cubic again at the point (-2, -8),
                            which is marked with a dot.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.62]
                                %% Axes
                                \draw[->,thin,gray!70] (-2.8,0) -- (2.8,0)
                                    node[right,black,font=\small] {$x$};
                                \draw[->,thin,gray!70] (0,-9.4) -- (0,9.4)
                                    node[above,black,font=\small] {$y$};
                                %% Tick marks
                                \foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2} {
                                    \draw[thin,gray!70] (\x,0.18) -- (\x,-0.18)
                                        node[below,font=\scriptsize,black] {$\x$};
                                }
                                \foreach \y in {-8,-4,4,8} {
                                    \draw[thin,gray!70] (0.18,\y) -- (-0.18,\y)
                                        node[left,font=\scriptsize,black] {$\y$};
                                }
                                %% Cubic y = x^3
                                \draw[blue!65!black, line width=1.2pt,
                                      domain=-2.1:2.1, samples=60]
                                    plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x});
                                \node[blue!65!black, right, font=\small] at (1.8,7.5)
                                    {$y=x^3$};
                                %% Tangent at (1,1): y = 3x - 2
                                \draw[orange!75!black, line width=1.2pt]
                                    (-2.35,{3*(-2.35)-2}) -- (2.4,{3*2.4-2});
                                \node[orange!75!black, right, font=\small]
                                    at (2.2,{3*2.2-2+0.2}) {$y=3x-2$};
                                %% Point of tangency (1,1)
                                \filldraw[blue!65!black] (1,1) circle (3.5pt)
                                    node[above right=1pt, font=\small] {$(1,1)$};
                                %% Second intersection (-2,-8)
                                \filldraw[orange!75!black] (-2,-8) circle (3.5pt)
                                    node[right=2pt, font=\small] {$(-2,-8)$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Show that the tangent at <m>x = a</m> has equation <m>y = 3a^2 x - 2a^3</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            By solving <m>x^3 = 3a^2 x - 2a^3</m>,
                            find the other <m>x</m>-value where the tangent meets the curve.
                            Express your answer in terms of <m>a</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Verify your result for <m>a = 1</m>: find the tangent at <m>x = 1</m>
                            and confirm where it meets <m>y = x^3</m> again.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>
                    In part (b), rearrange to <m>x^3 - 3a^2 x + 2a^3 = 0</m>.
                    Since the line is tangent at <m>x = a</m> (not just intersecting),
                    <m>x = a</m> is a <em>repeated</em> root. Factor out <m>(x - a)^2</m>.
                </p>
            </hint>
            <solution component="lesson-plan">
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f'(x) = 3x^2</m>, so the gradient at <m>x = a</m> is <m>f'(a) = 3a^2</m>
                            and the point is <m>(a, a^3)</m>.
                            <md>
                                <mrow>y \amp= 3a^2(x - a) + a^3</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 3a^2 x - 3a^3 + a^3</mrow>
                                <mrow>\amp= 3a^2 x - 2a^3</mrow>
                            </md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Setting <m>x^3 = 3a^2 x - 2a^3</m> gives <m>x^3 - 3a^2 x + 2a^3 = 0</m>.
                            Since the tangent touches the curve at <m>x = a</m>, it is a repeated root:
                            <md>x^3 - 3a^2 x + 2a^3 = (x - a)^2(x + 2a)</md>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            (Check by expanding: <m>(x-a)^2(x+2a) = (x^2 - 2ax + a^2)(x+2a)
                            = x^3 + 2ax^2 - 2ax^2 - 4a^2 x + a^2 x + 2a^3
                            = x^3 - 3a^2 x + 2a^3</m> ✓)
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            The other root is <m>x = -2a</m>.
                            The tangent at <m>x = a</m> meets the curve again at <m>x = -2a</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            At <m>a = 1</m>: tangent is <m>y = 3x - 2</m>.
                            The other intersection is at <m>x = -2</m>.
                            Check: <m>f(-2) = (-2)^3 = -8</m> and <m>3(-2) - 2 = -8</m>. ✓
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </solution>
            <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>See solution.</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>x = -2a</m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Tangent <m>y = 3x-2</m> meets <m>y=x^3</m> again at <m>(-2,-8)</m>. ✓</p></li>
                </ol>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-tangent-hyperbola-area">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The power rule works for negative exponents too: if <m>f(x) = x^n</m>
                    for any real number <m>n</m>, then <m>f'(x) = nx^{n-1}</m>.
                    In particular, for <m>y = \dfrac{1}{x} = x^{-1}</m>,
                    we have <md>\dfrac{dy}{dx} = -x^{-2} = -\dfrac{1}{x^2}</md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Consider the curve <m>y = \dfrac{1}{x}</m> for <m>x \gt 0</m>.
                    The diagram shows the case <m>x = 1</m>.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-hyperbola-tangent-triangle">
                    <caption>
                        The tangent to <m>y = \frac{1}{x}</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> cuts off a triangle
                        (shaded) from the first quadrant. Is the area always the same?
                    </caption>
                    <image xml:id="tikz-hyperbola-tangent-triangle" width="50%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Graph of y equals one over x in the first quadrant with an orange tangent
                            line at the point (1,1). The line runs from (0,2) on the y-axis to (2,0)
                            on the x-axis, enclosing a shaded triangle with the axes.
                            The area of the triangle is labelled as 2.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.35]
                                %% Shaded triangle
                                \fill[orange!12] (0,0) -- (2,0) -- (0,2) -- cycle;

                                %% Axes
                                \draw[->,thin,gray!70] (-0.2,0) -- (3.3,0)
                                    node[right,black,font=\small] {$x$};
                                \draw[->,thin,gray!70] (0,-0.2) -- (0,3.3)
                                    node[above,black,font=\small] {$y$};

                                %% Hyperbola y = 1/x
                                \draw[blue!65!black, line width=1.2pt,
                                      domain=0.31:3.1, samples=80]
                                    plot (\x, {1/\x});
                                \node[blue!65!black, right, font=\small]
                                    at (2.9,0.38) {$y=\tfrac{1}{x}$};

                                %% Tangent at (1,1): y = -x + 2
                                \draw[orange!80!black, line width=1.2pt] (0,2) -- (2,0);
                                \node[orange!80!black, font=\small] at (1.38,0.74)
                                    {$y=-x+2$};

                                %% Axis intercepts
                                \filldraw[orange!70!black] (2,0) circle (2.2pt)
                                    node[below right, font=\small] {$(2,0)$};
                                \filldraw[orange!70!black] (0,2) circle (2.2pt)
                                    node[left, font=\small] {$(0,2)$};

                                %% Point of tangency
                                \filldraw[blue!65!black] (1,1) circle (2.2pt)
                                    node[above right=1pt, font=\small] {$(1,1)$};

                                %% Axis ticks
                                \foreach \v in {1,2} {
                                    \draw[thin] (\v,0.07) -- (\v,-0.07)
                                        node[below,font=\small] {$\v$};
                                    \draw[thin] (0.07,\v) -- (-0.07,\v)
                                        node[left,font=\small] {$\v$};
                                }

                                %% Area label
                                \node[gray!60!black, font=\small] at (0.52,0.52)
                                    {Area $= 2$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the equation of the tangent to <m>y = \frac{1}{x}</m> at <m>x = 1</m>.
                        Find where it crosses the <m>x</m>-axis and the <m>y</m>-axis.
                        Find the area of the triangle formed by the tangent line, the <m>x</m>-axis,
                        and the <m>y</m>-axis.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                         At <m>x = 1</m>: gradient <m>= -\frac{1}{1^2} = -1</m>, point <m>(1, 1)</m>.
                        <md>y = -1(x - 1) + 1 = -x + 2</md>
                        <m>x</m>-intercept: <m>0 = -x + 2 \implies x = 2</m>. Point: <m>(2, 0)</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Repeat part (a) for <m>x = 2</m> and for <m>x = 3</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>y</m>-intercept: <m>x = 0 \implies y = 2</m>. Point: <m>(0, 2)</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        At <m>x = 2</m>: gradient <m>= -\frac{1}{4}</m>, point <m>(2, \frac{1}{2})</m>.
                        <md>y = -\tfrac{1}{4}(x - 2) + \tfrac{1}{2} = -\tfrac{x}{4} + 1</md>
                        <m>x</m>-intercept: <m>x = 4</m>. <m>y</m>-intercept: <m>y = 1</m>.
                        Area <m>= \frac{1}{2} \times 4 \times 1 = 2</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        At <m>x = 3</m>: gradient <m>= -\frac{1}{9}</m>, point <m>(3, \frac{1}{3})</m>.
                        <md>y = -\tfrac{1}{9}(x - 3) + \tfrac{1}{3} = -\tfrac{x}{9} + \tfrac{2}{3}</md>
                        <m>x</m>-intercept: <m>\frac{x}{9} = \frac{2}{3} \implies x = 6</m>.
                        <m>y</m>-intercept: <m>y = \frac{2}{3}</m>.
                        Area <m>= \frac{1}{2} \times 6 \times \frac{2}{3} = 2</m>.
                    </p>

                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What do you notice about the areas from parts (a) and (b)?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The area is always <m>2</m>, regardless of the point chosen.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Prove your observation: show that the tangent to <m>y = \frac{1}{x}</m>
                        at a general point <m>x = a</m> (where <m>a \gt 0</m>) always cuts off
                        a triangle of the same area from the first quadrant.
                        What is that area?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        At <m>x = a</m>: gradient <m>= -\frac{1}{a^2}</m>, point <m>(a, \frac{1}{a})</m>.
                        <md>
                            <mrow>y \amp= -\frac{1}{a^2}(x - a) + \frac{1}{a}</mrow>
                            <mrow>\amp= -\frac{x}{a^2} + \frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{a}</mrow>
                            <mrow>\amp= -\frac{x}{a^2} + \frac{2}{a}</mrow>
                        </md>
                        <m>x</m>-intercept: <m>0 = -\frac{x}{a^2} + \frac{2}{a} \implies x = 2a</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <m>y</m>-intercept: <m>x = 0 \implies y = \frac{2}{a}</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Area of triangle:
                        <md>\frac{1}{2} \times 2a \times \frac{2}{a} = \frac{1}{2} \times 4 = 2</md>
                        The area is always <m>2</m>, for any <m>a \gt 0</m>. ✓
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
            <!-- <answer>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Tangent <m>y = -x + 2</m>; intercepts <m>(2,0)</m> and <m>(0,2)</m>; area <m>= 2</m>.</p></li>
                    <li>
                        <p>At <m>x=2</m>: tangent <m>y = -\frac{x}{4}+1</m>, area <m>=2</m>.</p>
                        <p>At <m>x=3</m>: tangent <m>y = -\frac{x}{9}+\frac{2}{3}</m>, area <m>=2</m>.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li><p>The area is always <m>2</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For general <m>a</m>: intercepts <m>(2a, 0)</m> and <m>(0, \frac{2}{a})</m>; area <m>= \frac{1}{2} \cdot 2a \cdot \frac{2}{a} = 2</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </answer> -->
        </exercise>

    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Focus on Part A of the activity and Examples 1–2.
                        Provide a step card: (1) find <m>f'(x)</m>, (2) evaluate at <m>x=a</m>,
                        (3) find <m>f(a)</m>, (4) substitute into the formula.
                        Exercises 1 and 2 are sufficient.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line>Full activity, all three examples, and Exercises 1–6.</line>
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Exercises 7 and 8 both reveal beautiful hidden structure.
                        After Exercise 7, ask: does the same pattern hold for <m>f(x) = x^4</m>?
                        (The other intersection is at <m>x = -a</m>, a different relationship.)
                        After Exercise 8, ask: is the result still true for <m>x \lt 0</m>?
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

